 6. ✡️ Jewish institutions&#x27; security spending tops $1B 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 NYPD counterterrorism officers guard the entrance to Temple Emanu-El on the Upper East Side last year. Photo: Barry Williams/New York Daily News via Getty Images Antisemitic threats have pushed the nationwide cost of protecting Jewish synagogues, schools and community centers past $1 billion a year, new estimates reveal as congregations prepare for big crowds during the High Holy Days .

 The big picture: Enhanced security spending is often using up money that could pay for teachers, summer camps, elder care and other programs, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. By the numbers: More than 4,500 synagogues, Jewish community centers, schools, summer camps and other nonprofits spend an average of roughly $225,000 each on security, according to new data from the Jewish Federations of North America . 

 Security personnel — including guards and security directors — account for the largest share. State of play: Synagogues are increasingly moving beyond security guards and cameras to layered, year-round systems that include hardened entrances , trained congregants as security personnel and coordination with police.

 The expanded security comes as assaults linked to antisemitism have reached their highest level since the Anti-Defamation League began tracking them in 1979. Read on . 

 
 
