 7. 🇺🇸 America remembers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 People make a path as the "Wedge of Light," representing the Twin Towers, appears on the floor inside the Oculus in Manhattan yesterday. Photo: Heather Khalifa/AP A quarter century after  the 9/11 attacks killed nearly 3,000 people, Americans gathered at Ground Zero, the Pentagon, and Shanksville, Pa., where Flight 93 crashed, to mourn those lost.

 "[A]t the annual reading of the names ... the passage of time was palpable. Some 9/11 family members reading the names were so young when their parents were killed they said they had no memories of them. Others mourned grandparents, uncles and aunts they never met," The New York Times' Andy Newman writes . Image: The Wall Street Journal This was the cover of a Wall Street Journal special section yesterday.

 The Journal's newsroom, located directly across the street from the World Trade Center, was evacuated and heavily damaged when the towers fell. Throughout the rest of the day, WSJ reporters and editors walked the streets of Manhattan with their notebooks and gathered in each other's apartments to piece together the next day's paper. Watch a video . 
 
